Counting the Number of Set Bits :

[Brian Kernighan's Algorithm]
Approach:

Instead of checking every bit, we can use:

n & (n - 1)

This operation removes the rightmost set bit (1) from the number.

The idea:

Keep removing the last set bit.
Count how many times we perform this operation.
The number of operations will be equal to the number of set bits.

This is known as Brian Kernighan's Algorithm.

Example:

Given:

n = 13

Binary:

13 = 1101
Step 1:

Remove the rightmost set bit:

n     = 1101
n - 1 = 1100

Perform AND:

  1101
& 1100
------
  1100

New value:

1100

Count:

count = 1
Step 2:

Remove the rightmost set bit:

n     = 1100
n - 1 = 1011

AND:

  1100
& 1011
------
  1000

New value:

1000

Count:

count = 2
Step 3:

Remove the rightmost set bit:

n     = 1000
n - 1 = 0111

AND:

  1000
& 0111
------
  0000

New value:

0000

Count:

count = 3

Now n = 0, so the loop stops.

Answer:

13 has 3 set bits.
Why does n & (n-1) work?

Example:

n = 101100

The rightmost set bit:

101100
    ↑

Subtract 1:

101011

AND:

  101100
& 101011
--------
  101000

Only the rightmost 1 is removed.

Code:
class Solution {
public:
    int countSetBits(int n) {
        int count = 0;

        while (n) {
            n &= (n - 1);
            count++;
        }

        return count;
    }
};
Time Complexity: O(K)

Where K = number of set bits (1s) in the number.

Example:

n = 10000000

Only one 1 exists, so the loop runs once.

Space Complexity: O(1)

No extra space is used.

💡 Shortcut to remember:

n & (n-1) → removes the rightmost 1 bit

Example:

1101 → 1100 → 1000 → 0000

Number of removals = Number of set bits.
